#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087390 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n1. $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n2. $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n3. $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = 0$.\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$. Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n- Next, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = x^4$.\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = y$.\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n$y^{\\prime}+x^3y-y=0$, or $y^{\\prime}+(x^3-1)y=0$.\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$, the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + y\\sin x - \\cos x =0$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\sin x - \\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\sin x &= \\cos x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\underbrace{\\sin x}_{P(x)}y &= \\underbrace{\\cos x}_{Q(x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\sin x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\cos x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$x^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x)y = e^x$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x) \\,\n$$\n\nOn an interval where $x\\ne0$, dividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\sin (2x)y &= e^{x} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{x^2}y &= \\frac{e^{x}}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e9f778-66fa-54e7-acc6-001b43aaaaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} {:answer/id #uuid "c28a08c9-ed5f-57c9-a678-ebf32e926c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087116} {:answer/id #uuid "f09d6fcb-7508-53dc-9ebf-082a30a5867b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087117} {:answer/id #uuid "1046320f-9c93-59e8-ae7e-8dbe81821495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} =-\\frac{1}{x}y + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087118} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc89d23-a930-5ede-b341-6ac4428f6277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x - x\\frac{dy}{dx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e9f778-66fa-54e7-acc6-001b43aaaaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087115} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b999b3-f12d-5d5e-aa21-cfda0ea51796" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x - y}{x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, expand the right side as $y^{\\prime}=2-y/x$. Move the $y$ term to the left to obtain $y^{\\prime}+\\frac1x y=2$. This is standard form with $P(x)=1/x$ and $Q(x)=2$."}]}
